  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-3x+1)}=$",
  "worked_solution": "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-3x+1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\cdot e \\\\  & =e\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={e}^{-3x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943873/assets/c3d9e0bfa0b7514713c01289fc63fde7b0bd0dab525ad50617a9e908fd117060.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\ne\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{-3x}=e\\cdot 0=0\n$$",
